+
+## Definition
+
+Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice, written $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, as used by the Hilbert-space, trace-class, and supplied-basis data below. Use natural units $c=\hbar=1$. A **pure-point physical index datum** consists of a specified physical model and background, a complex Hilbert space $\mathcal H=\mathcal H^+\oplus\mathcal H^-$ as an orthogonal sum of closed subspaces, and the grading operator $\Gamma$ equal to $+I$ on $\mathcal H^+$ and $-I$ on $\mathcal H^-$. It also supplies a densely defined self-adjoint operator $\mathcal Q$ with domain invariant under $\Gamma$ and $\mathcal Q\Gamma\psi=-\Gamma\mathcal Q\psi$ on that domain. Set $K=\mathcal Q^2$ on $D(K)=\{\psi\in D(\mathcal Q):\mathcal Q\psi\in D(\mathcal Q)\}$ and require that $K$ is nonnegative and self-adjoint.
+
+Require an orthogonal pure-point decomposition
+
+$$\mathcal H=\mathcal H_0^+\oplus\mathcal H_0^-\oplus\widehat{\bigoplus}_{\lambda\in\Sigma}(\mathcal H_\lambda^+\oplus\mathcal H_\lambda^-),$$
+
+where $\Sigma\subset(0,\infty)$ is a supplied countable set of distinct eigenvalues, $\mathcal H_0^\pm=\ker K\cap\mathcal H^\pm$ are finite-dimensional, and each $\mathcal H_\lambda^\pm=\ker(K-\lambda I)\cap\mathcal H^\pm$ is finite-dimensional and contained in $D(K)$. A compatible orthonormal basis $E=E^+\sqcup E^-$ of these subspaces is supplied as part of the data. For each $\beta>0$, also supply a bounded diagonal operator $T_\beta$ with eigenvalue $1$ on $E\cap\ker K$ and $e^{-\beta\lambda}$ on $E\cap(\mathcal H_\lambda^+\oplus\mathcal H_\lambda^-)$, and require $T_\beta$ to be trace class. The parameter $\beta$ has units of inverse energy when $K$ has energy units; in dimensionless radial-quantization conventions it is dimensionless.
+
+The **Witten index** of this datum is the absolutely convergent graded heat supertrace
+
+$$W(\beta)=\operatorname{Tr}(\Gamma T_\beta)=\sum_{e\in E^+}\langle T_\beta e,e\rangle-\sum_{e\in E^-}\langle T_\beta e,e\rangle,\qquad \beta>0,$$
+
+where the sums are over finite subsets and then their absolutely convergent limits. The protected sector counted with signs is exactly $\ker K=\mathcal H_0^+\oplus\mathcal H_0^-$; the index is its even dimension minus its odd dimension, not its total degeneracy. A physical model may call this a **BPS index** only when its own supercharge algebra identifies this kernel with a specified BPS-saturated sector. Here a BPS-saturated state means a domain vector in a specified massive rest fiber satisfying the mass/central-charge equality in the earlier BPS definition. The present definition does not identify every BPS state with $\ker K$ and asserts no such state exists.
+
+## Definition references
+
+[Tong, §§1.2.1–1.2.2](https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/susy/susyqm.pdf)
+
+[Kinney et al., §3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0510251)
+
