## Example

Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ and use the right-handed index convention on the unit round $S^3$ from the earlier superconformal-index definition. Take an uncharged free four-dimensional $\mathcal N=1$ chiral model with trivial global gauge group, one complex scalar and one Weyl fermion in its stated chiral multiplet, no spatial boundary, and the directly supplied radial Hilbert/Fock construction; no path-integral measure is asserted. Its protected-letter data consist of one bosonic scalar letter $\phi$ of charges $(E,J_1,J_2,R)=(1,0,0,\tfrac23)$ and one fermionic conjugate Weyl letter $\bar\psi_{\dot +}$ of charges $(\tfrac32,0,\tfrac12,\tfrac13)$. Its protected one-particle space consists exactly of the towers $D_p^mD_q^n\phi$ and $D_p^mD_q^n\bar\psi_{\dot +}$ for $m,n\in\mathbb N$, where the commuting right-index-preserving derivatives $D_p,D_q$ each raise $E$ by $1$ and shift $(J_+,J_-)$ by $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$, respectively. Assume no additional $\Delta=0$ relations and no gauge projection. The protected multiparticle sector is the symmetric Fock construction with arbitrary finite occupation of each bosonic mode and the exterior Fock construction with zero or one occupation of each fermionic mode; any remaining Hilbert states are positive-gap pairs satisfying the beta-independence theorem and its trace-class conditions. For positive fugacities $0<p,q<1$, the single-letter index and full index are

$$i_\chi(p,q)=\frac{(pq)^{1/3}-(pq)^{2/3}}{(1-p)(1-q)},\qquad \mathcal I_\chi(p,q)=\prod_{m,n\ge0}\frac{1-(pq)^{2/3}p^mq^n}{1-(pq)^{1/3}p^mq^n},$$

where the double product is the limit of its finite rectangular partial products. These formulas describe the specified protected-letter model only; they assert no interacting or gauge-projected index.

## Example references

[Rastelli and Razamat, §2.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1608.02965)

[Kinney et al., §3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0510251)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The unit-radius right-handed index convention and the free protected-letter/Fock data stated in the Example; $p,q\in(0,1)$ are dimensionless.

[A1] Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, as required by the Hilbert, basis, and trace suppliers. [[def-countable-choice]]

[A2] The free model supplies exactly the stated protected scalar and fermion towers, with the two indicated $\Delta=0$ derivatives, symmetric/exterior Fock construction, no gauge projection, and no additional $\Delta=0$ relations. Any remaining Hilbert states are positive-gap pairs satisfying the beta-independence theorem and its trace-class conditions. This is model input for the example, not a theorem about a general SCFT.

[F1] [[def-qftx-superconformal-index]]: $\Delta=E-2J_2-\tfrac32R$, $J_+=J_1+J_2+R/2$, $J_-=J_2-J_1+R/2$, and the admissible weighted supertrace is the signed $p^{J_+}q^{J_-}$ trace on $\ker\Delta$ when its weighted diagonal sum converges.

[F2] [[pthm-qftx-superconformal-index-beta-independence]]: under exact positive-energy pairing and the weighted trace-class hypothesis, the full superconformal heat supertrace equals its absolutely convergent weighted trace on $\ker\Delta$ for every $\beta>0$.

## Verification

**Given:** [A1]–[A2], [F1]–[F2], and the charge assignments in the Example.

1.1 For the scalar letter, $\Delta=E-2J_2-\tfrac32R=1-0-1=0$ and $J_+=J_-=1/3$, so its signed fugacity weight is $(pq)^{1/3}$. [F1, A2, given, algebra]

1.2 For $\bar\psi_{\dot +}$, $\Delta=\tfrac32-1-\tfrac12=0$ and $J_+=J_-=\tfrac12+\tfrac16=2/3$; its fermion parity contributes the negative weight $-(pq)^{2/3}$. Each $D_p$ adds one to $J_+$ and zero to $J_-$, while each $D_q$ adds zero to $J_+$ and one to $J_-$, and both preserve $\Delta=0$. [F1, A2, given, algebra]

2.1 Summing the scalar and fermion towers gives $i_\chi(p,q)=\sum_{m,n\ge0}((pq)^{1/3}-(pq)^{2/3})p^mq^n$. Since $0<p,q<1$, the geometric sums are $\sum_{m,n\ge0}p^mq^n=((1-p)(1-q))^{-1}$, yielding the displayed single-letter formula. [step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]

3.1 For a bosonic letter of weight $x$, all occupations contribute $1+x+x^2+\cdots=(1-x)^{-1}$; for a fermionic letter, the two allowed occupations contribute $1-x$. Applying these factors to each derivative mode produces the rectangular partial product in the Statement. Theorem [F2] identifies this protected-sector Fock trace with the full superconformal heat supertrace, so no $\beta$ factor remains. [F2, A2, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 By [A1] and [F1], the trace is the supplied-basis trace of a trace-class weighted heat operator, with absolutely summable diagonal. Put $a=(pq)^{1/3}<1$ and $b=(pq)^{2/3}<1$. The total absolute bosonic and fermionic mode weights are $a/((1-p)(1-q))$ and $b/((1-p)(1-q))$, both finite. Because $-\log(1-x)\le x/(1-a)$ for $0\le x\le a$ and $\log(1+y)\le y$ for $y\ge0$, the absolute Fock partition product converges; hence the signed product and the protected-sector trace converge absolutely. This verifies the stated fugacity range for this model. [A1, F1, A2, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎

