        "library": "physics",
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$: every sequence of nonempty sets has a choice function. Use natural units $c=\\hbar=1$. A **pure-point physical index datum** consists of a specified physical model record and its operator data. The model record identifies the spacetime and spatial domain, metric and orientation, global gauge group including its global form, field bundles and representations, adopted action or other postulates, and boundary conditions; it also states whether the Hilbert-space operators are constructed directly or obtained from a named regulator and measure. These are supplied model inputs, not conclusions of this definition. When curved spacetime is used, the record includes fixed Riemannian spin geometry, spin and R-symmetry data, and the model-specific spinor equation as in the earlier background definition; no claim is made that arbitrary geometry admits such data. The operator data include a complex Hilbert space $\\mathcal H=\\mathcal H^+\\oplus\\mathcal H^-$ as an orthogonal sum of closed subspaces, and the grading operator $\\Gamma$ equal to $+I$ on $\\mathcal H^+$ and $-I$ on $\\mathcal H^-$. They supply a densely defined self-adjoint operator $\\mathcal Q$ with domain invariant under $\\Gamma$ and $\\mathcal Q\\Gamma\\psi=-\\Gamma\\mathcal Q\\psi$ on that domain. Set $K=\\mathcal Q^2$ on $D(K)=\\{\\psi\\in D(\\mathcal Q):\\mathcal Q\\psi\\in D(\\mathcal Q)\\}$ and require that $K$ is nonnegative and self-adjoint. The grading is dimensionless; if $K$ has energy units, $\\mathcal Q$ has energy dimension $1/2$.\n\nRequire an orthogonal pure-point decomposition\n\n$$\\mathcal H=\\mathcal H_0^+\\oplus\\mathcal H_0^-\\oplus\\widehat{\\bigoplus}_{\\lambda\\in\\Sigma}(\\mathcal H_\\lambda^+\\oplus\\mathcal H_\\lambda^-),$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma\\subset(0,\\infty)$ is a supplied countable set of distinct eigenvalues, $\\mathcal H_0^\\pm=\\ker K\\cap\\mathcal H^\\pm$ are finite-dimensional, and each $\\mathcal H_\\lambda^\\pm=\\ker(K-\\lambda I)\\cap\\mathcal H^\\pm$ is finite-dimensional and contained in $D(K)$. A compatible orthonormal basis $E=E^+\\sqcup E^-$ of these subspaces is supplied as part of the data. For each $\\beta>0$, also supply a bounded diagonal operator $T_\\beta$ with eigenvalue $1$ on $E\\cap\\ker K$ and $e^{-\\beta\\lambda}$ on $E\\cap(\\mathcal H_\\lambda^+\\oplus\\mathcal H_\\lambda^-)$, and require $T_\\beta$ to be trace class. The parameter $\\beta$ has units of inverse energy when $K$ has energy units; in dimensionless radial-quantization conventions it is dimensionless.\n\nThe **Witten index** of this datum is the absolutely convergent graded heat supertrace, defined by the supplied basis rather than by an unproved basis-independence claim,\n\n$$W_E(\\beta)=\\sum_{e\\in E^+}\\langle T_\\beta e,e\\rangle-\\sum_{e\\in E^-}\\langle T_\\beta e,e\\rangle,\\qquad \\beta>0,$$\n\nwhere each heat operator is trace class, meaning compact with summable singular values, and the supplied-basis diagonal sums are absolutely convergent; the sums are taken over finite subsets and then their absolutely convergent limits. This signed supertrace is not a raw state degeneracy. The zero-gap subspace singled out by the selected supercharge is $\\ker K=\\mathcal H_0^+\\oplus\\mathcal H_0^-$. Equality of the full heat supertrace with the even-minus-odd dimension of this subspace is a separate conditional theorem under the exact hypotheses. A physical model may call this a **BPS index** only when its own supercharge algebra identifies this kernel with a specified BPS-saturated sector. Here a BPS-saturated state means a domain vector in a specified massive rest fiber satisfying the mass/central-charge equality in the earlier BPS definition. The present definition does not identify every BPS state with $\\ker K$ and asserts no such state exists."
